  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 PM CDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nWe remain in a rather amplified pattern this afternoon across the \nCONUS. Broad and strong ridging is still in place over much of the \neastern CONUS, centered around the Mississippi River. Farther west, \nour main feature to watch is a deep upper low currently centered \nover the Great Basin. Surface observations show a weak low over \nwestern Iowa with a trailing cold front stretching back into south-\ncentral Nebraska. Warm southwesterly flow ahead of this front, \ncoupled with deep mixing, is leading to one more very warm day \nacross the entire area. More likely than not that both TOP and CNK \nwill break their daily high (and warm low) temperature records today \nas temperatures climb into the low/mid 90s.\n\nTonight into tomorrow, a lead shortwave moving onto the Plains will \nhelp shunt the cold front a bit farther south into our area. Have \ntrended this front a bit farther south in line with higher-\nresolution guidance, as this seems to be a classic slow bias in \nlower resolution guidance with southward moving fronts like this. \nStill some uncertainty with where exactly the front will stall out \nTuesday afternoon, but it looks to be somewhere plus/minus 30 miles \nfrom a McPherson to Council Grove to Topeka line. South of the front \nit will be one last very warm day with highs again approaching 90. \nNorth of the front it will be notably cooler, with highs in the 70s \nto low 80s. With good heating south of the boundary and convergence \nalong it, confidence is fairly high in thunderstorms developing \nduring the evening (likely beginning 5-7 PM). A few strong to severe \nstorms may occur with more elevated convection north of the boundary,\nbut the best chance for severe thunderstorms will be along and south\nof wherever the boundary ends up. Effective shear of 40+ kts and \n2000 J/kg of ML CAPE suggests that initial supercells with a \nhail/wind risk will be possible. However with a boundary-parallel \nshear vector, expecting rather quick upscale growth, transitioning \nthings more to a wind and heavy rain threat (given near record PWAT \nfor mid- October). An additional round of showers and storms should \ndevelop overnight into the morning hours, so it's not out of the \nquestion that a few spots pick up a quick 2-4\" of rain. However most \nplaces will see lesser amounts.\n\nThe storms Tuesday night will push the front through the majority of\nthe area. Temperatures Wednesday and Thursday look much cooler \nbehind the front, particularly if clouds and showers hang around. \nThere are signs of a brief lull in precipitation Wednesday PM, but \nrain coverage looks to increase again late Wednesday night into \nThursday. This will occur as the upper low lifts northeast onto the \nCentral Plains, becoming weaker and more sheared out as it interacts \nwith a trough dropping southeast out of Canada. Another few inches of\nrain will be possible with this round, particularly across east-\ncentral KS closest to the boundary to our south. So will have to \nwatch for some minor flooding concerns, particularly if the heaviest \nrain Thursday overlaps with the heaviest rain from Tuesday night.\n\nMuch drier air will move in Thursday night behind a secondary cold \nfront, as the now combined upper low and Canadian shortwave move off \nto the east. Strong high pressure will arrive in its wake. This will \nmake for a much more October-like weekend. Morning lows drop into \nthe 40s, likely even 30s Saturday morning as the high pressure \nbecomes situated overhead. And even with plenty of sun, high \ntemperatures should stay in the upper 50s to low 60s Friday and \nSaturday before climbing closer to 70 Sunday.",
